{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS HealthLake now supports CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F) requirements - AWS","context":"AWS HealthLake, a HIPAA-eligible service, has announced native support for healthcare payers to comply with the CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F). The rule mandates Medicare Advantage organizations, Medicaid managed care plans, CHIP managed care entities, and Qualified Health Plan (QHP) issuers to implement four standardized FHIR-based APIs by January 1, 2027.\n\nThe new capabilities include:\n\n1. **Patient Access API:** This API supports CARIN IG for Blue Button® 2.1.0, enabling patients to access their claims, encounter data, and prior authorization information through third-party applications. It also provides secure, standards-based authorization for patient-facing applications, drug coverage information query, and API use metrics collection for annual reporting.\n\n2. **Provider Access API:** This API allows sharing of patient claims and encounter data with in-network providers, supports payer-to-provider clinical data exchange, and provides drug formulary information to treating providers. It also includes consent management integration for patient opt-out workflows and provider attribution.\n\n3. **Payer-to-Payer API:** This API facilitates claims and encounter data exchange between payers, supports clinical data exchange across payer boundaries, and enables payers to identify shared members at scale for data exchange. It also includes consent management integration for patient opt-in workflows and various features like bulk FHIR data export, group discovery APIs, and prior authorization metrics support.\n\n4. **Prior Authorization API:** This API allows providers to query whether prior authorization is required and discover documentation requirements for items and services, supports compilation of necessary documentation to accompany prior authorization requests, and enables end-to-end electronic prior authorization including submission, status tracking, and decision communication via FHIR APIs. It also provides secure authorization for prior authorization workflow applications and prior authorization metrics support.\n\nAWS HealthLake is available in multiple regions, and customers are responsible for determining their own compliance obligations under CMS-0057-F and should consult with legal and compliance counsel regarding their specific requirements","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/aws-healthlake-cms-cms-0057-f/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-observability","skill_description":"Investigates root causes on the Omni context graph (service dependencies); builds/configures/debugs AWS observability - operator-symptom questions, detecting Omni vs classic. CloudWatch on a reporting service: fleet health, Log Insights (cost), metric/composite/anomaly alarms, custom metrics/EMF, dashboards, X-Ray/ADOT tracing, canaries, CloudTrail (incl. OTLP/PromQL), CloudWatch Profiles (CPU/memory hotspots, flame graphs), Dynamic Instrumentation (live breakpoints), Application Signals service map. CloudWatch Omni on an existing Space: SQL over logs/traces, PromQL, LLM inference (vLLM) diagnosis, Omni dashboards, alerts, Slack notifications, programmatic/IaC access, driving Omni from coding agents/skills, AI agent quality from traces - on-demand/online evals incl. setup, readback, evaluators. First-time setup (Omni Space, access grants, ingestion, ADOT instrumentation for Application Signals/Omni): use setting-up-cloudwatch-observability. Not for app logging or threat detection.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, cloudwatch-omni, xray, cloudtrail, synthetics, application-signals\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize, configure, enable, onboard, instrument, query\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability","skill_name":"aws-observability"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"finding-aws-customer-stories","skill_description":"Helps users work backwards from a business challenge by finding and verifying public AWS customer success stories: case studies, customer pages, AWS blog posts and press that name a customer, what it built and the outcome it reported. Matches stories to the user's industry, region, company size, partner and date window, labels how well each fits, quotes only verbatim text, flags services in maintenance or sunset, and says plainly when few or no stories exist. Triggers on: customer stories, case studies, success stories, reference stories, proof points, who else has done this, companies like us, examples of <industry> companies using AWS, inspiration before we build, customer quotes, stories for a pitch, slide, QBR or exec briefing. Does NOT apply to: reference architectures, Solutions, Guidance, sample code or how a service works (use aws-guide), building or configuring a service (use that service's skill), cost estimates (use estimating-aws-workload-costs).\n\n\nServices: *\nTasks: research, discover\nPersona: business-leader, technology-leader, builder, seller, partner-manager, account-manager, solutions-architect, marketing\nWorkload: general","skill_name":"finding-aws-customer-stories"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"creating-api-gateway-stage","skill_description":"Creates an API Gateway stage with CloudWatch logging, X-Ray tracing, throttling, WAF integration, and IAM roles following AWS best practices. Use when deploying a REST API to different environments such as dev, test, or production.\n\nServices: api-gateway, iam, cloudwatch, wafv2\nTasks: create, configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: serverless\n*","skill_name":"creating-api-gateway-stage"}]}}"}]}], "label": "No-op placeholder"}

